const questions=["Who is together when the newcomer arrives?","What rumors are mentioned, and how does the narrator signal they are unverified?","What does the young man actually reveal about his name?","What does taking up the guitar prepare readers to expect?","Why would it be inaccurate to summarize Picardía's later story as though it had already happened in this canto?"];
const intro={"n":20,"title":"Picardía Arrives: A New Singer at the Family Reunion","nums":[855],"summary":"Martín Fierro and his two sons celebrate being together again after ten years apart. An unfamiliar young man asks to join them and requests a blessing. The narrator reports that he has recently been seen in the area, that some people claim he came from the frontier and that rumors connect him with a pulpero's losses at horse races. These are rumors, not verified facts. Wearing poor clothing, he gives his name only as Picardía, asks permission to tell his history and takes up a guitar. The canto ends just as his song is about to begin.","people":"Martín Fierro; his two sons; the newcomer who calls himself Picardía; the gathered audience; unnamed people spreading rumors about him.","themes":"A new character and narrative voice; oral performance; identity and naming; rumors versus evidence; community reunion; transitions between storytellers.","words":"Picardía (the young man's chosen name, suggesting clever mischief); forastero (outsider); pulpero (keeper of a rural store); recado (horse tack); cantor (singer); templar las cuerdas (tune the strings).","questions":["Who is together when the newcomer arrives?","What rumors are mentioned, and how does the narrator signal they are unverified?","What does the young man actually reveal about his name?","What does taking up the guitar prepare readers to expect?","Why would it be inaccurate to summarize Picardía's later story as though it had already happened in this canto?"],"analysis":"The complete source canto is one long stanza. It provides the setting and introduces Picardía but does not yet tell the events of his life. The educational guide preserves that boundary and shows how the poem prepares for a change of speaker.","related":"/en/characters/","relatedTitle":"Character guide and narrative voices"};
